%YAML 1.1
%TAG !u! tag:unity3d.com,2011:
--- !u!114 &11400000
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 7c7c947f0ccabb24dbf991df8e9f6d4f, type: 3}
  m_Name: res tool characteristics
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  defaultQuestions:
  - QuestionText: What is the primary purpose of a research tool?
    Options:
    - To make the report look longer
    - To prove the researcher's hypothesis
    - To systematically collect data relevant to the research problem
    - To analyze data statistically
    CorrectOptionIndex: 2
    LevelName: res tool characteristics
  - QuestionText: Which of the following is a fundamental characteristic of a good
      research tool?
    Options:
    - Complexity
    - Reliability
    - High Cost
    - Subjectivity
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: res tool characteristics
  - QuestionText: 'The consistency or stability of a research tool in measuring a
      variable is known as:'
    Options:
    - Validity
    - Reliability
    - Objectivity
    - Usability
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: res tool characteristics
  - QuestionText: 'The accuracy of a research tool in measuring what it is intended
      to measure is called:'
    Options:
    - Validity
    - Reliability
    - Practicability
    - Durability
    CorrectOptionIndex: 0
    LevelName: res tool characteristics
  - QuestionText: 'If a weighing scale shows different weights for the same person
      in a short span of time, the tool lacks:'
    Options:
    - Validity
    - Reliability
    - Objectivity
    - Clarity
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: res tool characteristics
  - QuestionText: 'If a test designed to measure mathematical reasoning only measures
      arithmetic calculation speed, it lacks:'
    Options:
    - Construct Validity
    - Test-Retest Reliability
    - Practicability
    - Economy
    CorrectOptionIndex: 0
    LevelName: res tool characteristics
  - QuestionText: 'The characteristic of a research tool that ensures it is easy
      to administer, score, and interpret is its:'
    Options:
    - Validity
    - Reliability
    - Practicability
    - Measurability
    CorrectOptionIndex: 2
    LevelName: res tool characteristics
  - QuestionText: 'A research tool that produces similar results when scored by different
      evaluators is said to have high:'
    Options:
    - Internal Consistency
    - Inter-rater Reliability
    - Split-half Reliability
    - Face Validity
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: res tool characteristics
  - QuestionText: Which type of validity is concerned with how well a test correlates
      with an external criterion?
    Options:
    - Content Validity
    - Criterion Validity
    - Construct Validity
    - Face Validity
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: res tool characteristics
  - QuestionText: 'The extent to which a tool appears to measure what it is supposed
      to measure is called:'
    Options:
    - Content Validity
    - Criterion Validity
    - Construct Validity
    - Face Validity
    CorrectOptionIndex: 3
    LevelName: res tool characteristics
  - QuestionText: 'A good research tool must be:'
    Options:
    - Vague and general
    - Precise and unambiguous
    - Based on personal opinion
    - Difficult to obtain
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: res tool characteristics
  - QuestionText: 'The characteristic that allows a research tool to be used with
      different groups and in different situations is its:'
    Options:
    - Cost-effectiveness
    - Generality
    - Length
    - Complexity
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: res tool characteristics
  - QuestionText: The "Test-Retest" method is used to establish which type of reliability?
    Options:
    - Internal Consistency
    - Stability over time
    - Equivalence
    - Inter-rater
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: res tool characteristics
  - QuestionText: 'The "Split-Half" method is used to assess:'
    Options:
    - Internal Consistency
    - Stability
    - Equivalence
    - Inter-rater
    CorrectOptionIndex: 0
    LevelName: res tool characteristics
  - QuestionText: Which characteristic ensures that a research tool is free from
      the personal bias of the scorer?
    Options:
    - Reliability
    - Objectivity
    - Validity
    - Sensitivity
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: res tool characteristics
  - QuestionText: How does reliability relate to validity?
    Options:
    - A tool can be valid without being reliable.
    - A tool must be reliable to be valid.
    - Reliability and validity are the same.
    - Validity is not important if a tool is reliable.
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: res tool characteristics
  - QuestionText: Why is "clarity of instruction" a vital characteristic of a research
      tool?
    Options:
    - To make the tool look professional.
    - To ensure all participants understand the task in the same way, enhancing reliability
      and validity.
    - To increase the difficulty.
    - To allow for subjective interpretation.
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: res tool characteristics
  - QuestionText: What is the purpose of establishing "norms" for a research tool?
    Options:
    - To make the tool more expensive.
    - To provide a standard frame of reference for interpreting an individual's score.
    - To hide the true purpose of the test.
    - To ensure it is only used once.
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: res tool characteristics
  - QuestionText: 'A tool that can detect small but important differences in the
      characteristic being measured is said to have high:'
    Options:
    - Reliability
    - Sensitivity
    - Objectivity
    - Generality
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: res tool characteristics
  - QuestionText: 'The "Kuder-Richardson" and "Cronbach''s Alpha" coefficients are
      measures of:'
    Options:
    - Internal Consistency Reliability
    - Test-Retest Reliability
    - Inter-rater Reliability
    - Criterion Validity
    CorrectOptionIndex: 0
    LevelName: res tool characteristics
  - QuestionText: 'A researcher wants to ensure their questionnaire covers all aspects
      of "job satisfaction." They are concerned with:'
    Options:
    - Criterion Validity
    - Content Validity
    - Construct Validity
    - Face Validity
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: res tool characteristics
  - QuestionText: 'A new depression scale is administered at the same time as a well-established
      scale. The correlation between them establishes:'
    Options:
    - Predictive Validity
    - Concurrent Validity
    - Construct Validity
    - Face Validity
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: res tool characteristics
  - QuestionText: "Assertion (A):\_A highly practical research tool is not necessarily
      valid.\_Reason (R):\_A tool can be easy to use but may not accurately measure
      the intended construct."
    Options:
    - Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.
    - Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A.
    - A is true but R is false.
    - A is false but R is true.
    CorrectOptionIndex: 0
    LevelName: res tool characteristics
  - QuestionText: Contrast "Reliability" and "Validity" using an archery target analogy.
    Options:
    - Reliability is a tight cluster; Validity is hitting the bullseye.
    - Reliability is a tight cluster of arrows; Validity is hitting the bullseye
      with that cluster.
    - Validity is a tight cluster; Reliability is hitting the bullseye.
    - They are the same in the analogy.
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: res tool characteristics
  - QuestionText: A tool that yields a score of 100, 102, and 98 for a stable characteristic
      is _____, whereas a tool that yields 150, 149, and 151 is _____.
    Options:
    - Valid, Reliable
    - Reliable but not valid, Reliable and potentially valid
    - Valid but not reliable, Neither
    - Neither, Both
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: res tool characteristics
  - QuestionText: 'The characteristic of a research tool that allows it to be used
      in various cultures after proper adaptation is its:'
    Options:
    - Objectivity
    - Cross-cultural Validity/Fairness
    - Economy
    - Sensitivity
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: res tool characteristics
  - QuestionText: Why is "standardization" a critical process for a research tool?
    Options:
    - To make it unique.
    - To establish uniform procedures for administration and scoring, which enhances
      reliability and comparability.
    - To keep it a secret.
    - To make it subjective.
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: res tool characteristics
  - QuestionText: 'A tool that is inexpensive and time-efficient to use possesses
      the characteristic of:'
    Options:
    - Sensitivity
    - Economy
    - Generality
    - Objectivity
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: res tool characteristics
  - QuestionText: 'The ability of a tool to measure an abstract, theoretical construct
      (like intelligence) is known as:'
    Options:
    - Content Validity
    - Criterion Validity
    - Construct Validity
    - Face Validity
    CorrectOptionIndex: 2
    LevelName: res tool characteristics
  - QuestionText: 'A good research tool should have clear "operational definitions"
      to ensure:'
    Options:
    - That the variables are defined in measurable terms, enhancing clarity and replicability
    - That the tool is long enough
    - That the results are always significant
    - That it is based on theory alone
    CorrectOptionIndex: 0
    LevelName: res tool characteristics
  - QuestionText: Which of the following is the STRONGEST characteristic for ensuring
      a tool measures the correct concept?
    Options:
    - High Face Validity
    - High Construct Validity
    - High Inter-rater Reliability
    - Low Cost
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: res tool characteristics
  - QuestionText: 'A flaw in a research tool is indicated by:'
    Options:
    - Clear instructions
    - Vague or double-barreled questions that confuse respondents
    - A pilot study
    - Standardized scoring
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: res tool characteristics
  - QuestionText: 'The concept of "reactivity" refers to a situation where:'
    Options:
    - The tool is reliable.
    - The act of measurement itself alters the phenomenon being studied.
    - The tool is too long.
    - The scorer is biased.
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: res tool characteristics
  - QuestionText: 'A "ceiling effect" occurs when a tool is:'
    Options:
    - Too difficult for all participants
    - Too easy, so it fails to discriminate between high-ability individuals
    - Perfectly valid
    - Perfectly reliable
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: res tool characteristics
  - QuestionText: 'The "floor effect" is the opposite, occurring when a tool is:'
    Options:
    - Too difficult, so it fails to discriminate between low-ability individuals
    - Too easy
    - Perfectly sensitive
    - Highly objective
    CorrectOptionIndex: 0
    LevelName: res tool characteristics
  - QuestionText: 'The process of "pilot testing" a research tool is done to:'
    Options:
    - Identify and rectify ambiguities and assess its reliability and validity
    - Replace the main study
    - Guarantee significant results
    - Make the tool more theoretical
    CorrectOptionIndex: 0
    LevelName: res tool characteristics
  - QuestionText: 'For a tool to be considered "standardized," it must have:'
    Options:
    - Been used in many studies
    - Established norms, uniform administration procedures, and scoring rules
    - Been developed by a famous researcher
    - A high price tag
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: res tool characteristics
  - QuestionText: 'The characteristic of "comprehensiveness" ensures that a tool:'
    Options:
    - Is very long
    - Adequately covers the entire domain of the construct being measured
    - Is easy to score
    - Is used internationally
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: res tool characteristics
  - QuestionText: 'A tool that produces a normal distribution of scores in a large
      population is likely to have good:'
    Options:
    - Discriminatory Power
    - Face Validity
    - Economy
    - Reactivity
    CorrectOptionIndex: 0
    LevelName: res tool characteristics
  - QuestionText: 'The "standard error of measurement" is an index of:'
    Options:
    - Validity
    - The amount of error inherent in an individual's observed score
    - The tool's cost
    - The tool's length
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: res tool characteristics
  - QuestionText: 'A "culture-fair" test is designed to maximize:'
    Options:
    - Complexity
    - Validity across different cultural and subcultural groups
    - The difficulty level
    - Subjectivity
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: res tool characteristics
  - QuestionText: 'The term "parsimony" in the context of research tools refers to
      the:'
    Options:
    - Use of complex language
    - Principle that a tool should be as simple as possible while still effectively
      measuring the construct
    - Length of the tool
    - Cost of development
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: res tool characteristics
  - QuestionText: 'A key ethical characteristic of a research tool is that it should:'
    Options:
    - Be deceptive
    - Respect the dignity and rights of participants (e.g., confidentiality, minimal
      intrusion)
    - Be mandatory for all
    - Have no instructions
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: res tool characteristics
  - QuestionText: 'The "utility" of a research tool is a composite of its:'
    Options:
    - Color and design
    - Practical value, which depends on its reliability, validity, and practicability
    - Age and history
    - Theoretical origins
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: res tool characteristics
  - QuestionText: 'A tool that is "unobtrusive" has the advantage of minimizing:'
    Options:
    - Reactivity
    - Reliability
    - Validity
    - Cost
    CorrectOptionIndex: 0
    LevelName: res tool characteristics
  - QuestionText: 'When a tool''s items are all strongly related to the central construct,
      it demonstrates high:'
    Options:
    - Homogeneity
    - Heterogeneity
    - Objectivity
    - Generality
    CorrectOptionIndex: 0
    LevelName: res tool characteristics
  - QuestionText: 'The process of establishing the "validity" of a tool is:'
    Options:
    - A one-time event
    - An ongoing, cumulative process that requires multiple lines of evidence
    - Unnecessary if it is reliable
    - Only important for questionnaires
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: res tool characteristics
  - QuestionText: 'A good research tool should have appropriate "difficulty level"
      and "discriminatory index" to ensure it is:'
    Options:
    - Too hard for everyone
    - Suitable for the target population and can distinguish between different levels
      of the trait
    - Easy to fake
    - Based on old theories
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: res tool characteristics
  - QuestionText: 'The most critical characteristic that a researcher must establish
      for their tool before collecting main study data is:'
    Options:
    - Its color
    - Its reliability and validity for the intended population and purpose
    - Its popularity
    - Its length
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: res tool characteristics
  - QuestionText: 'The ultimate test of a research tool''s quality is its:'
    Options:
    - Price
    - Ability to yield trustworthy and meaningful data that accurately addresses
      the research questions
    - Complexity
    - Number of pages
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: res tool characteristics
